 },
 "protocol": {
  "ood": "all 160 windows; window-bootstrap 95% CI (2000 reps, seed 0) - OPTIMISTIC (resampling windows is not resampling domains)",
  "in_domain": "IndEgo 150 (7 clips); clip-bootstrap 95% CI; LOCO variants fit the normaliser on the held-out clip's own unlabelled values only",
  "fitting": "label-free: target statistics use X only; OOD labels never used for fitting",
  "determinism": "integer Q=4 comparisons, fixed constants, no RNG on the decision path"
 },
 "limitations": [
  "n_bad = 22 on OOD-160: every OOD AUROC has a wide CI; differences below ~0.05 are not resolvable.",
  "Window-bootstrap CIs are optimistic: windows are not independent (many share a source factory/batch and pairs share the same source video), so true sampling error is larger.",
  "Variants b/b2/c/d/e are TRANSDUCTIVE: they use the target domain's own unlabelled feature values (the whole target for b/b2/c/d). That is legitimate unlabelled domain adaptation, but it is not a purely inductive single-window decision: a deployed window must be scored against a bank of target statistics. Variant e (32-window calibration slice) quantifies how much of the effect survives with a small unlabelled slice.",
  "In-domain numbers for the raw table are in-sample (thresholds were derived on these 150 windows); only the LOCO variants are held-out, and their per-clip target domain is tiny (6-48 windows), so their quantile estimates are noisy.",
  "phys.snr_db is non-finite for 2/160 OOD and 3/150 IndEgo windows; the scorer skips non-finite features (that rule cannot fire on those windows), exactly as tools/phys.",
  "Reject/BA numbers use the frozen reject_threshold = 14 for every variant; the score distribution changes with re-quantiling, so the operating point is not re-optimised (that is deliberate: re-optimising on OOD would use OOD labels)."
 ],
